es  with her offspring  is via  breast milk  when she is feeding her baby and that's  going to be  iga or immunoglobulin a again if you're  not familiar with igg or iga or the  other types of antibodies check out my  video on antibody structure and function  just for some more detail there  but so this is called natural passive  immunity because it's a very natural  process right every person who's ever  been born  has received antibodies from their  mother when they were in their mother's  uterus  any child who has been breastfed will  have received  antibodies from their mother during the  the act of breastfeeding so that's the  natural passive immunity and now we can  talk about the second type that's the  artificial passive immunity artificial  passive immunity is when antibodies  that are typically produced in a  laboratory  are injected into an individual  to prevent or fight a specific infection  now this is also known as passive  inoculation  now i said at the beginning passive  immunity and passive inoculation are not  interchangeable terms  passive inoculation is another word for  this artificial passive immunity so it's  one type of passive immunity  now here before we move on i want to  make a couple of points clear  one  is that the artificial  passive immunity has to do with passing  antibodies from one individual to  another  in a way that involves some kind of  medical procedure basically  so the antibodies can either be produced  in a laboratory or i should have written  this on the board also i'll i'll write  it right here produced in a laboratory  or  um  [Music]  donated  as plasma  so if you've heard of someone donating  blood um you can also donate plasma  during the covid19 pandemic i think  we've heard a lot about convalescent  plasma and that's plasma  donated by a person who had previously  had cova 19 and had fought off that  infection and so would have antibodies  in their blood that they could donate  so the antibodies here they're either  the ones that are going to be donated  that's the convalescent plasma or  produced in a laboratory another major  treatment for covid19 especially later  in the pandemic has been with specific  monoclonal antibodies i'm going to put  that in parentheses here so this would  be the kind that's produced in a  laboratory monoclonal  antibodies  but either way whether i know people  personally in my life who received  convalescent plasma when they were  hospitalized with covet 19 from from a  donor  and i know people who received a  monoclonal antibody treatment  in the hospital when they were sick with  probit 19 later  later in the pandemic so all both of  those are types of artificial passive  immunity so it's where you have  antibodies that were either produced in  a lab or donated by just some donor out  there that are then  injected and really the method here when  i say injected i don't mean like an  intramuscular shot i mean this is an iv  administration so administered  intravenously  right into like a vein  um so an iv  administration of these antibodies into  an individual either to prevent right it  could be like a prophylactic treatment  so to prevent them from contracting a  certain disease perhaps  just for example if you if somebody was  exposed to a person who is ill they  might be administered  um like monoclonal antibodies  prophylactically so to prevent a disease  or to fight off a specific infection if  they had already tested positive for it  and so that is the passive inoculation  part  now in either form of passive immunity  so either  natural or artificial  the transferred antibodies whether it's  coming from a mother to her offspring  via placenta or breast milk or whether  it was an intravenous administration of  monoclonal antibodies or convulsive  plasma either way  antibodies have a half-life they don't  last forever  and so  those transferred antibodies  will be  to get kind of low here  degraded  okay will be degraded over time  so degraded over time um typically this  is several months you know three to six  months  kind of depends but um basically once  the antibodies have been  received  um they they only last  several months and once that time  has passed they will have been degraded  and that immunity that passive immunity  will have been  eliminated unless more antibodies are  transferred  [Music]  so passive immunity tends to be  transient whereas active immunity this  full immune response particularly  because it's got the memory cells it can  last decades last an entire lifetime  because there are those memory cells  ready and waiting to recognize that  pathogen again and to mount an immune  response if it comes  so  now let's take a look at this diagram  we've got the innate and adaptive arms  of immunity  within the adaptive arm there's the  artificial and the natural like we've  been talking about and for both  artificial and natural we have active  and passive components i'm just going to  summarize those here  artificial  active  this is going to generate memory and  this is what would happen  for example from a vaccine  so a vaccine is artificial in the sense  that it's not like a natural infection  but it's very active so it's going to  result in full immunity you know a  person who gets a vaccine  is going to have b cells and t cells and  antibodies and memory cells to  to fight off the pathogen and to  remember it in the future  so that's the that artificial active  component and yes there's memory with  that  the artificial  passive component is like what we were  talking about right here so this is  where you've got like convalescent  plasma or i'm actually going to write  just as the example here  monoclonal antibodies  there's no memory  but it can be a good way to increase  immunity for a temporary amount of time  so that's the artificial passive looking  at the natural end of things the active  natural situation that results in memory  this would be  such as during an infec